-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/ee7b900c-a6fc-58be-b5fe-cd1ada9ee2d5
-- title:
--   An element of mathbb T_θ interpolating the U_q-eigenvalues ± 1
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a characteristic-zero discrete valuation domain, complete for its maximal ideal, with finite residue field $k$, let $p$ be a prime with $p \neq 2$ and $p \in \mathfrak m_{\mathcal O}$, and let $\bar\rho$ be a residual representation over $k$ (a two-dimensional $k$-space with a multiplicative action of $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ factoring through a finite level) whose base change to $\overline k$ is irreducible. Let $S$ be a finite set of primes containing $p$, and $N \neq 0$ an integer all of whose prime divisors lie in $S$, with the weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ spanned over $\mathbb C$ by their integral lattice. Let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism to $k$ from the Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb C}$ of the cusp forms generated by the $T_\ell$ ($\ell$ prime, $\ell \nmid N$, $\ell \notin S$) and the $U_{q'}$ ($q'$ prime, $q' \mid N$, $q' \notin S$), and assume that for every prime $\ell \nmid N$ with $\ell \notin S$, every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\ell \in P$ a nonunit and every $\sigma$ which is a Frobenius at $\ell$ for $P$, the characteristic polynomial of $\bar\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2 - \theta(T_\ell)X + \ell$. Let $q$ be a prime with $q \neq p$, $q \mid N$, $q^2 \nmid N$, such that $\bar\rho$ is ramified at $q$, i.e. some $P$ over $q$ has an inertia element acting non-trivially. Then there exists $u$ in [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131), the localisation of the base-changed Hecke lattice at the complement of the prime kernel of $\theta$, with the following property. Let $\mathcal O'$ be another characteristic-zero complete discrete valuation domain with finite residue field which is a module-finite $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\mathcal O \to \mathcal O'$ local, let $\psi$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism from [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131) to $\mathcal O'$, let $M \neq 0$ divide $N$, and let $g$ be a weight-two cusp form on $\Gamma_0(M)$ which is a newform (a normalised eigenform whose eigensystem does not already occur at any proper divisor of $M$). Suppose $\chi_g$ is a ring homomorphism from the level-$M$ Hecke algebra away from $S$ to $\mathbb C$ with $\chi_g(T_\ell)$ the $\ell$-th $q$-expansion coefficient of $g$ for all primes $\ell \nmid M$, $\ell \notin S$, and $\iota$ is a ring homomorphism from the range of $\chi_g$ to $\mathcal O'$ with $\iota(\chi_g(T_\ell)) = \psi(\pi(T_\ell))$ for all primes $\ell \nmid N$, $\ell \notin S$, where $\pi$ is the canonical map of the Hecke algebra into [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S 𝒪 θ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131). Then $q \mid M$, and there is an integer $a = \pm 1$ with the $q$-th $q$-expansion coefficient of $g$ equal to $a$ in $\mathbb C$ and $\psi(u) = a$ in $\mathcal O'$.
--
--   This is the Galois-theoretic half of the assertion that $U_q$ is redundant in the localised Hecke algebra when $\bar\rho$ is ramified at a prime $q$ exactly dividing the level (Carayol's local-global compatibility at $q$, in the form used by Wiles, Proposition 2.15, and in the proof of Proposition 4.7 of Darmon–Diamond–Taylor): it produces a single element of the anemic local algebra whose value under each arithmetic point is the $U_q$-eigenvalue $\pm 1$ of the corresponding newform. It feeds the identification of that element with the image of $U_q$, the construction of idempotent splittings with an auxiliary prime, and the multiplicity-one count of residual root multiplicities.
